nt is $2.$ So, any rational roots of $f(x)$ must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of }-4}{\\text{factor of }2}.\n$$\n\nOf the given answer choices, only $-3$ does not take the above form. The value $-3$ can be interpreted as a fraction $-\\frac{3}{1},$ and the numerator $(3)$ is not a factor of $-4.$" 13194139533969 true] [17592186275317 1000 #edb/ref 17592186275318 13194139533969 true] [17592186275317 1000 #edb/ref 17592186275319 13194139533969 true] [17592186275317 1000 #edb/ref 17592186275320 13194139533969 true] [17592186275317 1000 #edb/ref 17592186275321 13194139533969 true] [17592186275317 1000 #edb/ref 17592186275322 13194139533969 true] [17592186275317 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139533969 true] [17592186275317 1041 "selection" 13194139533969 true] [17592186275317 1104 #edb/ref 17592186275320 13194139533969 true] [17592186275317 1110 #uuid "5cfc2450-6b19-591a-9c9e-b5e95faf3ba4" 13194139533969 true] [17592186275318 1034 "-\\frac{1}{2}" 13194139533969 true] [17592186275318 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533969 true] [17592186275318 1111 #uuid "6db0cb1c-ddd2-5d21-93a2-77d908629596" 13194139533969 true] [17592186275319 1034 "2" 13194139533969 true] [17592186275319 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533969 true] [17592186275319 1111 #uuid "ed59bdbb-62c4-542b-8280-0eb3578c2cfb" 13194139533969 true] [17592186275320 1034 "-3" 13194139533969 true] [17592186275320 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533969 true] [17592186275320 1111 #uuid "45db3c94-38e5-5ad3-a37c-7f0e88992089" 13194139533969 true] [17592186275321 1034 "4" 13194139533969 true] [17592186275321 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533969 true] [17592186275321 1111 #uuid "00900e9b-3910-5b72-9916-acb816889f25" 13194139533969 true] [17592186275322 1034 "-1" 13194139533969 true] [17592186275322 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533969 true] [17592186275322 1111 #uuid "b9bc64bd-bfeb-5d48-a031-fe0aa87cbfd6" 13194139533969 true] [17592186275323 1017 #edb/ref 17592186275324 13194139533969 true] [17592186275323 1089 "q-112984" 13194139533969 true] [17592186275323 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139533969 true] [17592186275323 1100 "Given that the polynomial $f(x)=4{x}^{3}-15{x}^{2}+12x+4$ has a root in the interval $(-\\frac{1}{2},0),$ find the sum of the **distinct** real roots of $f(x).$" 13194139533969 true] [17592186275323 1113 #uuid "bf13e900-4dce-558a-80db-3ec5cf963787" 13194139533969 true] [17592186275323 1145 "Given a polynomial with integer coefficients, the rational roots theorem states that any rational roots of the polynomial must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of the constant term}}{\\text{factor of the leading coefficient}}.\n$$\n\nFor the given polynomial $f(x),$ the constant term is $4$ and the leading coefficient is $4.$ So, any rational roots of $f(x)$ must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of }4}{\\text{factor of }4}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the possible rational roots are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{1}{4},\\,\\pm \\frac{1}{2},\\,\\pm 1,\\,\\pm 2,\\,\\pm 4.\n$$\n\nOf these roots, the only root in the interval $(-\\frac{1}{2},0)$ is $-\\frac{1}{4}.$\n\nUsing the synthetic division, we get the following:\n\n|  | ${x}^{3}$ | ${x}^{2}$ | ${x}^{1}$ | ${x}^{0}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-\\frac{1}{4}$ | $4$ | $-15$ | $12$ | $4$ |\n|  | $↓$ | $-1$ | $4$ | $-4$ |\n|  | $4$ | $-16$ | $16$ | $0$ |\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\nf(x)=(x+\\frac{1}{4})(4{x}^{2}-16x+16).\n$$\n\nSince the highest-degree factor is now a quadratic, we can continue factoring using the usual methods:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =(x+\\frac{1}{4})(4{x}^{2}-16x+16) \\\\  & =4(x+\\frac{1}{4})({x}^{2}-4x+4) \\\\  & =(4x+1)(x-2)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the factors equal to $0,$ we find the roots $x=-\\frac{1}{4},2.$ Summing these roots, we get\n\n$$\n-\\frac{1}{4}+2=\\frac{7}{4}.\n$$" 13194139533969 true] [17592186275324 1000 #edb/ref 17592186275325 13194139533969 true] [17592186275324 1000 #edb/ref 17592186275326 