oefficients, the rational roots theorem states that any rational roots of the polynomial must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of the constant term}}{\\text{factor of the leading coefficient}}.\n$$\n\nFor the given polynomial:\n\n- The constant term is $-18,$ and its factors are $\\pm 1,\\,\\pm 2,\\,\\pm 3,\\,\\pm 6,\\,\\pm 9$ and  $\\pm 18.$\n\n- The leading coefficient is $1,$ and its factors are $\\pm 1.$\n\nFirst, we list out all the possibilities for the numerator and denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\pm \\frac{1}{1}, & \\pm \\frac{2}{1}, & \\pm \\frac{3}{1}, & \\pm \\frac{6}{1}, & \\pm \\frac{9}{1}, & \\pm \\frac{18}{1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we reduce the fractions to lowest terms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\pm 1, & \\pm 2, & \\pm 3, & \\pm 6, & \\pm 9, & \\pm 18.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that none of these potential roots are duplicates. \n\nThis makes a total of $12$ potential roots. (Remember that each $\\pm $ sign accounts for $2$ roots, and $2\\cdot 6=12.$)" 13194139533969 true] [17592186275331 1000 #edb/ref 17592186275332 13194139533969 true] [17592186275331 1000 #edb/ref 17592186275333 13194139533969 true] [17592186275331 1000 #edb/ref 17592186275334 13194139533969 true] [17592186275331 1000 #edb/ref 17592186275335 13194139533969 true] [17592186275331 1000 #edb/ref 17592186275336 13194139533969 true] [17592186275331 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139533969 true] [17592186275331 1041 "selection" 13194139533969 true] [17592186275331 1104 #edb/ref 17592186275332 13194139533969 true] [17592186275331 1110 #uuid "0f485854-2381-534f-af7c-b4632b31f533" 13194139533969 true] [17592186275332 1034 "12" 13194139533969 true] [17592186275332 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533969 true] [17592186275332 1111 #uuid "c47db36c-d5d4-5ebc-ba45-5f9e0e650fda" 13194139533969 true] [17592186275333 1034 "10" 13194139533969 true] [17592186275333 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533969 true] [17592186275333 1111 #uuid "5a51eefa-f12c-5bce-ae4b-5b